  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>In the south of the town, right next to Fort de Brégançon (the French presidents' summer residence since 1968, its silhouette visible to the left facing the sea), Cabasson is a fine-sand beach backed by small vegetated dunes that shelter it from the wind. To the right, the coastal path leads on to l'Estagnol.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>According to the town hall's website (ville-bormes.fr, page updated 13 March 2025), from 15 June to 30 September dogs on leash are only allowed "on the part of Cabasson beach near the Fort (east side of the rocky breakwater)" and on Gouron beach — in practice, the small <a href="/en/fiche-bormes-les-mimosas">Fort de Brégançon beach</a> right next door, which has its own listing. Only assistance dogs are allowed on all the municipal beaches. For the rest of the year the town hall gives no other rule; the tourist office (2025 holiday booklet) cites dogs allowed from November to March, while its online page on Cabasson says "animals not accepted" without specifying the season. For April, early June and October, check with the tourist office (04 94 01 38 38).</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: in high summer, don't just settle anywhere on Cabasson with your dog — head straight for the small Fort beach, on the east side of the rocky breakwater, the only zone allowed during that period.</div>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p><a href="/en/fiche-bormes-les-mimosas">Fort de Brégançon beach</a> is right next door (same car park). The <a href="/en/fiche-balade-cabasson-estagnol">coastal path to l'Estagnol</a> starts from this same car park.</p>
    </div>

